WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.320 --> 00:00:05.570
In the 1980s and 1990s, the charismatic and
photogenic "Muscles from Brussels" became

00:00:05.570 --> 00:00:09.960
an international movie star — and then,
just as fast and unlikely as his rise, came

00:00:09.960 --> 00:00:10.460
the fall.

00:00:10.860 --> 00:00:16.660
Here's how the man born Jean-Claude Camille
François Van Varenberg has occupied himself

00:00:16.660 --> 00:00:17.660
in recent years.

00:00:18.520 --> 00:00:24.039
Two of the biggest hits of Van Damme’s early
career were Universal Soldier, a 1992 sci-fi

00:00:24.039 --> 00:00:28.970
action thriller where he played a deceased
veteran resurrected as a technologically enhanced

00:00:28.970 --> 00:00:34.080
super-soldier, and 1989’s Kickboxer, where
he played… a kickboxer.

00:00:34.660 --> 00:00:39.820
Both films wound up spawning full-on franchises,
and while Van Damme himself wasn’t always

00:00:39.829 --> 00:00:43.500
involved, he did eventually return to the
roles that made him famous.

00:00:43.500 --> 00:00:49.199
In 1999, Van Damme reunited with Dolph Lundgren,
along with Michael Jai White and pro wrestler

00:00:49.200 --> 00:00:53.680
Bill Goldberg, for the appropriately named
Universal Soldier: The Return.

00:00:54.060 --> 00:00:59.059
A decade later, he returned again for 2009’s
Universal Soldier: Regeneration and while

00:00:59.059 --> 00:01:04.420
that movie ignored the previous film entirely,
it led to Lundgren and Van Damme closing out

00:01:04.420 --> 00:01:07.090
the franchise with Day of Reckoning in 2012.

00:01:07.240 --> 00:01:12.560
As for Kickboxer, that franchise became a
straight-to-video juggernaut with five installments

00:01:12.560 --> 00:01:13.660
by 1995.

00:01:14.200 --> 00:01:19.790
Unfortunately for Van Damme fans (or Fan Dammes),
those movies opted to replace him with leading

00:01:19.790 --> 00:01:26.400
men like Step By Step’s Sasha Mitchell and
future Iron Chef America star Mark Dacoscas.

00:01:26.640 --> 00:01:32.020
After 20 years, however, the franchise returned
for 2016’s Kickboxer: Vengeance, and Van

00:01:32.030 --> 00:01:37.040
Damme reappeared in the series, and even fought
Drax the Destroyer! and it seems to have

00:01:37.040 --> 00:01:41.560
been a good enough time that he returned for
the seventh installment, Kickboxer: Retaliation.

00:01:41.960 --> 00:01:44.980
In that one, JCVD fights Mike Tyson.

00:01:45.380 --> 00:01:46.260
No, really.

00:01:46.780 --> 00:01:49.680
“You mention Mongo one more time and I’ll
smash your face.

00:01:50.620 --> 00:01:51.120
“...really?”

00:01:52.460 --> 00:01:55.160
Those weren’t his only brushes with franchises,
though.

00:01:55.380 --> 00:02:00.330
In 2010, he was personally offered a role
in The Expendables by Sylvester Stallone,

00:02:00.330 --> 00:02:01.080
but turned it down.

00:02:01.420 --> 00:02:05.740
According to an interview with Van Damme,
it was because of commitments to another movie,

00:02:05.740 --> 00:02:09.720
but Stallone claimed it was because he objected
to a scene where his character would lose

00:02:09.720 --> 00:02:11.040
a fight against Jet Li.

00:02:11.440 --> 00:02:15.860
Fortunately, there weren’t any hard feelings,
and Stallone found a place for Van Damme in

00:02:15.860 --> 00:02:16.860
The Expendables 2.

00:02:17.380 --> 00:02:22.340
Unless you count the usual blood and bruises
that result from on-screen fistfights, Van

00:02:22.340 --> 00:02:25.900
Damme has rarely appeared onscreen with his
face obscured by makeup.

00:02:26.220 --> 00:02:30.570
There was, however, a time that he almost
took on one of the most costume-heavy roles

00:02:30.570 --> 00:02:34.280
in Hollywood when he was cast as the title
monster in The Predator.

00:02:34.560 --> 00:02:39.420
Unfortunately for the famously athletic actor,
the heavy suit restricted his movement to

00:02:39.420 --> 00:02:44.600
the point of making a thrilling martial arts
battle against Arnold Schwarzenegger impossible.

00:02:44.870 --> 00:02:49.420
At the time of his departure, the Predator
looked very different from its final version,

00:02:49.430 --> 00:02:53.730
and Van Damme would later explain that while
he was in the “very dangerous type of outfit,”

00:02:53.730 --> 00:02:55.980
he was “moving everything with cables.”

00:02:56.160 --> 00:03:00.510
It was far too limited to make any good use
of his skills, so he walked after only two

00:03:00.510 --> 00:03:05.200
days of production, leaving Arnold to duke
it out with a redesigned alien who did significantly

00:03:05.200 --> 00:03:07.780
less kickboxing than called for in the original
draft.

00:03:09.020 --> 00:03:13.770
Van Damme is famously nicknamed "The Muscles
from Brussels," as he was indeed a ripped

00:03:13.770 --> 00:03:16.360
guy born in the capital city of Belgium.

00:03:16.360 --> 00:03:22.500
In 2012, Brussels paid tribute to one of its
favorite sons with a life-sized bronze statue,

00:03:22.670 --> 00:03:26.860
forever posed in a fight-ready stance in front
of the Westland Shopping Center.

00:03:26.860 --> 00:03:31.640
A visibly touched Van Damme unveiled the statue
himself at a special ceremony, saying the

00:03:31.640 --> 00:03:32.640
monument ...

00:03:32.740 --> 00:03:34.920
"represented the dream of a Brussels kid"

00:03:35.240 --> 00:03:38.500
… and hoping it could serve as a source
of inspiration for troubled youth.

00:03:39.220 --> 00:03:44.160
If you’ve seen Hard Target, you already
know JCVD hates snakes.

00:03:44.380 --> 00:03:45.280
Dogs, though?

00:03:45.520 --> 00:03:46.960
The dude loves dogs.

00:03:47.280 --> 00:03:52.520
In 2016, Van Damme appeared at a fundraiser
for Animals Australia, encouraging people

00:03:52.520 --> 00:03:57.420
to adopt retired racing dogs after greyhound
racing was banned in New South Wales.

00:03:57.790 --> 00:04:03.300
He's even worked on behalf of endangered species,
meeting with Australia's Environment Minister

00:04:03.300 --> 00:04:08.660
to help procure funding for relocating endangered
rhinos, gorillas, and elephants to Australia.

00:04:09.120 --> 00:04:12.910
On top of those efforts, he’s trying to
put together a foundation of wealthy individuals

00:04:12.910 --> 00:04:14.820
to create sanctuaries for those animals.

00:04:15.340 --> 00:04:19.240
Believe it or not, Van Damme didn’t hit
the peak of his popularity with movies like

00:04:19.240 --> 00:04:19.740
Bloodsport.

00:04:20.440 --> 00:04:24.340
Instead, the most notable recent moment in
his career came in the form of a commercial

00:04:24.340 --> 00:04:28.220
for Volvo that went viral thanks to Van Damme’s
signature move.

00:04:28.600 --> 00:04:34.180
One of the most iconic images of JCVD’s
career came in Timecop, when he leapt on a

00:04:34.180 --> 00:04:37.560
counter while doing the splits to avoid an
untimely demise.

00:04:37.980 --> 00:04:43.000
He did the same thing in the ad except this
time, the 53 year-old performer did them while

00:04:43.000 --> 00:04:46.840
balanced atop two giant trucks…driving backward.

00:04:47.080 --> 00:04:52.380
More than 86 million people have watched that
amazing clip which is more than the number

00:04:52.380 --> 00:04:56.260
of people who ever bought tickets to any single
film in his considerable career.

00:04:57.000 --> 00:05:01.400
Jean-Claude's daughter Bianca Van Damme is
certainly not someone you would want to mess

00:05:01.410 --> 00:05:06.220
with she’s been studying martial arts for
over 20 years, and once said she wanted to

00:05:06.220 --> 00:05:10.920
be a role model for kids by showing them they
can “kick ass in a nice, feminine way."

00:05:11.290 --> 00:05:16.140
Growing up, she hated martial arts, and was
forced into it at the age of seven by her

00:05:16.140 --> 00:05:20.200
mother, bodybuilder Gladys Portugues, in order
to help with self-discipline.

00:05:20.730 --> 00:05:24.440
Her interests at the time lay more on the
side of speed-skating and ballet one of her

00:05:24.440 --> 00:05:28.080
father’s passions as well but an injury
forced her into another path.

00:05:28.560 --> 00:05:32.640
She embraced martial arts and worked alongside
her father in several movies.

00:05:32.920 --> 00:05:37.780
In 1992, the producers of Universal Soldier
approached the Midway company with the hopes

00:05:37.780 --> 00:05:39.420
of making a game based on the movie.

00:05:39.990 --> 00:05:43.540
Midway wasn’t interested, but they did want
to work with the film’s star, Jean-Claude

00:05:43.550 --> 00:05:47.330
Van Damme, and worked out a deal with him
to make a game that would be something more

00:05:47.330 --> 00:05:49.940
along the lines of Kickboxer or Bloodsport.

00:05:50.280 --> 00:05:54.160
Unfortunately, the deal fell through, and
they never finished working on that project.

00:05:54.660 --> 00:06:00.520
Instead, that idea eventually lead to Mortal
Kombat complete with a character based on

00:06:00.520 --> 00:06:02.580
Van Damme in the form of Johnny Cage.

00:06:02.860 --> 00:06:07.800
Along with sharing a few of their initials,
Cage was also a martial arts movie star.

00:06:08.060 --> 00:06:12.800
Obviously, the licensing deal fell through
and the character was modeled off another

00:06:12.800 --> 00:06:17.490
actor, leaving Johnny Cage more of an homage
to the Muscles from Brussels than a direct

00:06:17.490 --> 00:06:18.260
representation.

00:06:18.580 --> 00:06:23.440
Still, knowing what could’ve been makes
us wish that we had gotten a bloody, ultra-violent

00:06:23.450 --> 00:06:27.930
game about the genuine article… or at least
seen Van Damme playing Cage in the Mortal

00:06:27.930 --> 00:06:28.880
Kombat movie.

00:06:29.340 --> 00:06:32.720
He probably would’ve been a better fit for
that role than the fighting game character

00:06:32.720 --> 00:06:37.660
he did take on for the screen, the mega-patriotic
Colonel Guile in Street Fighter.

00:06:38.020 --> 00:06:42.640
Van Damme has faced plenty of villains onscreen,
but his toughest battle in real life was against

00:06:42.640 --> 00:06:43.140
addiction.

00:06:43.600 --> 00:06:49.180
He has admitted to having a 10-gram-per-day
habit, which cost him around $10,000 a week.

00:06:49.500 --> 00:06:55.580
By 1999, he had racked up a DUI charge, and
while he tried rehab, he left after only seven

00:06:55.580 --> 00:06:56.080
days.

00:06:56.080 --> 00:07:00.200
A few years later, he relapsed, and found
himself in a pretty dark period of his career.

00:07:00.420 --> 00:07:06.740
From 1999 to 2008, Van Damme starred in 14
films, and even his most die-hard fans would

00:07:06.740 --> 00:07:08.360
have trouble remembering most of them.

00:07:08.860 --> 00:07:13.960
The string of generic direct-to-video action
flicks and Turkish heist movies earned him

00:07:13.960 --> 00:07:20.420
the nickname “Jean-Claude Van Desperate,”
but they also paved the way for 2008’s JCVD.

00:07:20.840 --> 00:07:24.820
That movie, in which he played a version of
himself who was caught up in a hostage crisis

00:07:24.820 --> 00:07:28.900
and confronted the emotional and somewhat
depressing aspects of his life as a has-been

00:07:28.900 --> 00:07:30.620
action star was well received.

00:07:30.760 --> 00:07:35.240
Along with kicking his cocaine habit, it helped
to bring him back to mainstream Hollywood.

00:07:35.240 --> 00:07:39.480
Van Damme’s substance abuse problem has
been linked with his bipolar disorder.

00:07:39.740 --> 00:07:45.220
His condition went undiagnosed for years before
finally being identified as Rapid Cycling

00:07:45.220 --> 00:07:49.020
Bipolar Disorder, which is often seen in people
who struggle with addiction.

00:07:49.280 --> 00:07:54.060
Before being medicated, he would try to literally
fight through the dark times by focusing on

00:07:54.060 --> 00:07:57.980
training, saying that when he didn’t hit
the gym, quote, "nothing could make [him]

00:07:57.980 --> 00:07:58.480
happy."

00:07:58.700 --> 00:08:02.860
The drugs were another attempt at happiness,
but obviously was not the solution to his

00:08:02.860 --> 00:08:03.360
problems.

00:08:03.620 --> 00:08:07.640
Eventually, his extensive cocaine use even
left him feeling suicidal.

00:08:07.920 --> 00:08:11.760
The good news is that he was able to battle
through his intense depression, and has since

00:08:11.760 --> 00:08:16.340
been very open about his disorder, both in
interviews and in some gut-wrenching scenes

00:08:16.340 --> 00:08:17.520
in JCVD.

00:08:17.780 --> 00:08:21.800
Over the past few years, he’s been able
to move forward, and deal with his condition

00:08:21.800 --> 00:08:23.840
in a far less self-destructive fashion.

00:08:24.560 --> 00:08:29.280
While he rose to meteoric fame in Hollywood
with a starring role in Bloodsport, Van Damme

00:08:29.280 --> 00:08:32.340
didn’t just get off the bus and walk right
onto a movie set.

00:08:32.760 --> 00:08:36.790
Long before he broke into the film industry,
he was an active fighter, with a record of

00:08:36.790 --> 00:08:41.760
18 wins and one loss in kickboxing winning
every single contest by knockout.

00:08:41.920 --> 00:08:47.480
In semi- and light-contact battles, his record
stands at 44 and 4, so his skills in the ring

00:08:47.480 --> 00:08:48.460
are pretty well known.

00:08:48.880 --> 00:08:52.760
Many have attributed his flowing fighting
style and graceful movement to his years of

00:08:52.770 --> 00:08:57.520
training in a different physical activity:
ballet, which he studied for five years while

00:08:57.520 --> 00:08:59.500
also earning his black belt in karate.

00:08:59.720 --> 00:09:04.260
That said, at least one person thinks that
Van Damme should’ve stuck to the dance floor,

00:09:04.270 --> 00:09:08.740
and while that’s an easy opinion to dismiss,
this one comes from someone who really ought

00:09:08.740 --> 00:09:10.380
to know: Frank Dux.

00:09:10.820 --> 00:09:13.140
If that name sounds familiar, it should.

00:09:13.260 --> 00:09:19.130
Dux was the guy Van Damme played in Bloodsport,
whose alleged real-life battles in the kumite

00:09:19.130 --> 00:09:20.600
were dramatized for the film.

00:09:21.000 --> 00:09:25.620
Dukes even worked as a fight choreographer
on the movie, and according to him, Van Damme

00:09:25.620 --> 00:09:27.220
quite simply can’t fight.

00:09:27.700 --> 00:09:33.830
In 1997, the Las Vegas sun reported that Dux
was suing Van Damme for $50,000, which was

00:09:33.830 --> 00:09:39.120
later upped to 1.5 million, claiming that
the star never paid him for his work as a

00:09:39.120 --> 00:09:41.840
co-writer on the 1996 film The Quest.

00:09:42.060 --> 00:09:46.200
During the suit, he took a dig at Van Damme’s
fighting abilities, claiming that when he

00:09:46.200 --> 00:09:50.850
was training him for Bloodsport, Van Damme
proved to be unable to perform even simple

00:09:50.850 --> 00:09:51.340
stunts.

00:09:51.360 --> 00:09:56.340
Oh, and as for his legitimate kickboxing career
with all those knockouts?

00:09:56.350 --> 00:10:00.890
According to Dux, "Van Damme … lied to the
public that he was a martial arts champion.”

00:10:00.890 --> 00:10:05.680
Unfortunately for Dux, a jury cleared Van
Damme, saying that the star didn’t owe anyone

00:10:05.680 --> 00:10:06.400
anything.

